@dataclass(kw_only=True)
class ComparableRankingType:
    """
    Used to compare different items (Task, Capability Command, Capability
    Activity, etc.).

    This is accomplished by first comparing two ComparableRankingTypes'
    priority values. In the event of the items having equal priority values
    the precedence values are then compared. For example, Task A has a
    (priority, precedence) value pair of (4, 10), Task B has a (priority,
    precedence) value pair of (4, 23), Task C has a (priority, precedence)
    value pair of (7, 2): the ranking order would be first Task A, then
    Task B, then Task C. Since Task A and Task B have the same relative
    priority value, Task A will be ranked before Task B because Task A has
    a higher relative precedence value. Task C will be ranked after Tasks A
    and B because it has a lower relative priority value than Tasks A and
    B.

    :ivar priority: Priority value of an item (Task, Capability Command,
        Capability Activity, etc.).  Any item with a numerically lower
        priority value must be considered prior to any items with a
        numerically higher priority value.  Priority doesn't necessarily
        dictate temporal ordering.  A value of zero is highest priority,
        next highest is one, etc. Priority changes monotonically. For
        example, given values A=6, B=4, C=3, the order should be first
        C, then B, then A, with C being the highest relative priority.
        This element follows the priority ranking approach where lower
        values are higher priority and higher values are lower priority.
    :ivar precedence_within_priority: Precedence value of an item (Task,
        Capability Command, Capability Activity, etc.).  Any item with a
        numerically lower precedence value must be considered prior to
        any items with a numerically higher precedence value.
        Precedence doesn't necessarily dictate temporal ordering.  A
        value of zero is highest precedence, next highest is one, etc.
        Precedence changes monotonically. For example, given values A=6,
        B=4, C=3, the order should be first C, then B, then A, with C
        being the highest relative precedence. This element follows the
        priority ranking approach where lower values are higher priority
        and higher values are lower priority.
    """

    priority: int = field(
        metadata={
            "name": "Priority",
            "type": "Element",
            "namespace": "https://www.vdl.afrl.af.mil/programs/oam",
        }
    )
    precedence_within_priority: None | int = field(
        default=None,
        metadata={
            "name": "PrecedenceWithinPriority",
            "type": "Element",
            "namespace": "https://www.vdl.afrl.af.mil/programs/oam",
        },
    )


