class LosVariableAType:
    """
    Defines a line of sight ray in a world inertial or body frame of
    reference.

    :ivar reference: Specifies whether the sibling Azimuth and Elevation
        elements are referenced to a body, NED, or inertial frame.
    :ivar azimuth: Indicates the azimuth from an observation point to a
        point of interest. When the sibling Reference element indicates
        INERTIAL, azimuth is defined as the angle between two lines in a
        global horizontal plane at the observation point. The first line
        is from the observation point to true north. The second line is
        from the observation point to the point of interest projected
        onto the global horizontal plane. An azimuth of zero indicates
        true north with values increasing clockwise. When the sibling
        Reference element indicates BODY, azimuth is defined as the
        angle between two lines in the plane containing the observer's
        roll and pitch axis (the "RP" plane). The first line is from the
        observation point along the observer's roll axis or "down the
        nose" in aircraft terminology. The second line is from the
        observation point to the point of interest projected onto the RP
        plane. An azimuth of zero indicates directly ahead of the
        observer ("down the nose") with values increasing clockwise. An
        azimuth of Pi/2 indicates directly right of the observer ("down
        the right wing"). The terms azimuth and bearing are often used
        interchangeably but they are technically different. Azimuth
        values are angles between 0 and 2*Pi radians (0 and 360 degrees)
        relative to reference. Bearing values are a combination of a
        reference direction (north or south) and an angle between 0 and
        Pi radians (0 and 180 degrees) towards the east or west
        direction. For example, an azimuth of Pi/2 radians (90 degrees)
        is equivalent to a bearing of "North 90 East" or "South 90
        East". UCI uses azimuth because it is easier for
        machine/software use.
    :ivar elevation: Indicates the elevation from an observation point
        to a point of interest. When the sibling Reference element
        indicates INERTIAL, elevation is defined as the angle between
        two lines in a global vertical plane at the observation point.
        The first line originates from the observation point and extends
        along the azimuth. The second line is from the observation point
        to the point of interest. An elevation of zero indicates the
        point of interest is in the observation point's horizontal
        plane. Elevation values are positive when the point of interest
        is above the horizontal plane, negative when below. When the
        sibling Reference element indicates BODY, elevation is defined
        as the angle between two lines in a plane containing the
        observer's yaw axis. The first line originates from the
        observation point and extends along the azimuth. The second line
        is from the observation point to the point of interest. An
        elevation of zero indicates the point of interest is in the
        observer's roll-pitch (RP) plane. Elevation values are positive
        when the point of interest is above the RP plane, negative when
        below.
    """

    class Meta:
        name = "LOS_VariableA_Type"

    reference: LosReferenceEnum = field(
        metadata={
            "name": "Reference",
            "type": "Element",
            "namespace": "https://www.vdl.afrl.af.mil/programs/oam",
        }
    )
    azimuth: float = field(
        metadata={
            "name": "Azimuth",
            "type": "Element",
            "namespace": "https://www.vdl.afrl.af.mil/programs/oam",
            "min_inclusive": 0.0,
            "max_inclusive": 6.283185307179586,
        }
    )
    elevation: float = field(
        metadata={
            "name": "Elevation",
            "type": "Element",
            "namespace": "https://www.vdl.afrl.af.mil/programs/oam",
            "min_inclusive": -3.141592653589793,
            "max_inclusive": 3.141592653589793,
        }
    )


