@dataclass(kw_only=True)
class ConflictType:
    """
    See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this
    type for details.

    :ivar conflict_id: Unique ID for this conflict.
    :ivar conflicting_object_id: The object that is causing the
        conflicted system to be in a contingency state.  This item is
        optional if and only if the conflicting object is not known.
        For example, when a FAILED Task is received by a parent system
        (FAILED task state is used to escalate the issue to the parent
        node).  This may generate a contingency alert at the parent node
        where the conflicting object is unknown.  In other words, a
        parent system receives a FAILED Task, but doesn't necessarily
        know why the Task has failed.  This could cause the parent
        system to generate an alert with a sibling AffectedRequirement
        without a ConflictingObject.
    :ivar conflict_location: This is the first location along the
        planned route where the conflict will occur. This data is
        optional and primarily applies to conflicts with pop-up threats
        or zones where the conflict occurs at a future point along the
        route. It is not expected that this data will be populated for
        Capability and other System failures which affect a System's
        ability to perform its assigned Tasking. The primary intent of
        this data is for display purposes to provide operator SA.
    :ivar conflict_time: Indicates the time window in which the conflict
        is expected to occur.
    :ivar affected_requirements: Indicates the ID of Requirement or
        Requirements which can no longer be performed due to the effect
        of the conflicting object. IDs are given if and only if they can
        no longer be accomplished with the current mission plans. As a
        result, a contingency generated by a pop-up threat (or other
        environment change) might be handled via a simple reroute which
        does not affect any Requirements. Alternatively, if a Capability
        fails, affected Requirements are more likely known and should be
        included in this element.  A Service would only list the
        Requirements if the Service KNOWS there is a problem with it.
        For example, a Service should not populate this value with all
        Tasks in an affected MissionPlan simply because it is uncertain
        as to the effect of a reroute on the associated Tasks.
    """

    conflict_id: ConflictIdType = field(
        metadata={
            "name": "ConflictID",
            "type": "Element",
            "namespace": "https://www.vdl.afrl.af.mil/programs/oam",
        }
    )
    conflicting_object_id: None | IdType = field(
        default=None,
        metadata={
            "name": "ConflictingObjectID",
            "type": "Element",
            "namespace": "https://www.vdl.afrl.af.mil/programs/oam",
        },
    )
    conflict_location: None | ConflictLocationType = field(
        default=None,
        metadata={
            "name": "ConflictLocation",
            "type": "Element",
            "namespace": "https://www.vdl.afrl.af.mil/programs/oam",
        },
    )
    conflict_time: None | DateTimeRangeType = field(
        default=None,
        metadata={
            "name": "ConflictTime",
            "type": "Element",
            "namespace": "https://www.vdl.afrl.af.mil/programs/oam",
        },
    )
    affected_requirements: None | RequirementsReferenceType = field(
        default=None,
        metadata={
            "name": "AffectedRequirements",
            "type": "Element",
            "namespace": "https://www.vdl.afrl.af.mil/programs/oam",
        },
    )


